# Lesson 1, Sample C. Example hand, plays itself. Follows Sample D.
title: How trumps work (4♥)
id: L01-C
type: example
lesson: 1
concepts: 1.2
student: S
board: 1
dealer: N
vulnerable: none

deal:
  N  K4     AKJ95  A7     Q652
  E  AQJ93  T7     J84    K83
  S  82     Q843   K952   JT4
  W  T765   62     QT63   A97

auction:
  N 1H     = North dealt, so North calls first. 1♥ offers to take 7 tricks (1 + 6) with hearts as trumps.
  E 1S     = Spades rank above hearts, so East can bid at the same level.
  S 2H     = Hearts rank below spades, so to bid hearts South must go up a level.
  W 2S
  N 4H     = A bid may skip levels. 4♥ offers to take 10 tricks.
  E Pass   = Three passes in a row end the auction.
  S Pass
  W Pass

auction recap:
  The contract is 4♥, meaning that NS have agreed to take 10 tricks (4+6)
  with hearts as trumps. So the objective of EW is to "set" the contract by
  taking 4 or more tricks themselves. North is declarer, because North was first
  on the side to name hearts. South also bid hearts, but later. East, on
  declarer's left, makes the opening lead. South becomes dummy.

play:
  1   E SA  S S2  W S5  N S4
      note: East leads face up, then South lays dummy on the table. Everyone follows suit. The ace is highest, so East wins and leads again.
  2   E SQ  S S8  W S6  N SK
      note: The king beats the queen. North wins, and leads next. Dummy has no spades left.
  3   N H5  E H7  S HQ  W H2
      note: Declarer leads trumps to take the opponents' trumps away, starting with dummy's queen so that the next trump can be won back in declarer's hand.
  4   S H3  W H6  N HA  E HT
      note: T stands for the ten. The opponents have no trumps left.
  5   N C2  E C3  S CJ  W CA
      note: West wins with the ace, so West leads next.
  6   W D3  N D7  E D4  S DK
      note: Dummy wins, so declarer's next card comes from dummy.
  7   S C4  W C7  N CQ  E CK
      note: East wins with the king.
  8   E SJ  S H8  W S7  N C5
      note: Dummy has no spades, so declarer may play any card. A trump wins the trick: a ruff. North has no spades either and throws away a club: a discard.
      pause
  9   S CT  W C9  N C6  E C8
      note: Dummy won the last trick, so dummy leads.
  10  S D2  W DT  N DA  E D8
      note: Dummy leads a diamond to declarer's ace.
  11  N HK  E S3  S H4  W D6
      note: Only North-South have trumps left, so each one is a sure winner.
  12  N HJ  E S9  S D5  W ST
      note: Declarer's last trumps are winners.
  13  N H9  E DJ  S D9  W DQ
      note: 10 tricks: 4♥ made.

play recap:
  Declarer's plan: draw the opponents' trumps, then set up club tricks. Once
  trumps are gone, the defenders can't ruff, and dummy's trumps stop their
  spades.

  North-South took 10 tricks and East-West took 3. Notice the pattern: the
  winner of each trick leads to the next; everyone follows suit when they
  can; and a player who can't follow may ruff with a trump or discard.
  Declarer plays both hands, but always in turn, clockwise.
